this morning we're learning about a  surprising comeback for a controversial  food ingredient the seasoning known as  MSG it used to be widespread but fell  out of favor over perceived health  concerns now research tells us many of  those concerns are unfounded and the  fears may be rooted in xenophobia rather  than science CBS correspondent Michael  George spoke with Two Chefs who cook  with MSG about what they say are  misconceptions and they also have some  clever ways that it's being used today  at this Arlington Virginia takeout  restaurant lucky Danger  Chef Tim Ma's secret ingredient isn't  really a secret MSG is very little  it's a seasoning with a controversial  history many Americans grew up hearing  it's unhealthy or even dangerous to  consume but now some like ma are  challenging that idea I will say I think  every dish has it in there we actually  sit it here on the walk station because  it is the last component of every dish  that goes out dishes like Kung Pao  chicken and dumplings ma was born into a  family of immigrants with a legacy of  Chinese cuisine his parents started  Bamboo Garden in Arkansas and his  uncle's restaurant Palmas China Kitchen  was featured in a Smithsonian exhibit on  Chinese food in America being a cook or  being a chef was very easy for them to  fall into and a way for them to make a  living but Ma's family never used MSG no  MSG was a badge of honor in a Chinese  restaurant there was a lot of stigma  like anti-asian sentiment around that  and the MSG just like fell into that  chef and author J Kenji Lopez alt says  the safety of MSG or monosodium  glutamate first came into question in  1968 when a doctor wrote a letter to the  New England Journal of Medicine titled  Chinese restaurant syndrome this wasn't  a published study there was a letter  written to the journal by a doctor  saying hey I've noticed when I go to  Chinese restaurants and eat MSG rich  foods I get these certain symptoms after  others reported similar symptoms an MSG  backlash began some critics targeted  Chinese restaurant owners at a time when  anti-asian sentiment was high the  Chicago Tribune even ran this headline  the whole idea has some racist  undertones as well because Chinese  restaurants had a reputation for being  sort of low quality food or dirtier food  some people still claim to experience  symptoms such as headaches or drowsiness  after eating foods containing MSG but  recent scientific research shows that  when consumed in moderation MSG is  harmless  in culinary practice MSG is a flavor  booster we all know the taste salty  bitter sweet and sour MSG triggers the  fifth taste Umami a sort of savoriness  or meatiness while MSG is often  associated with Asian Cuisine it's long  been an ingredient in American foods  from Campbell's soups and Heinz Ketchup  to KFC and Chick-fil-A you never hear  somebody say I have Doritos syndrome or  KFC syndrome if you don't hear about it  as like a specific term the way Chinese  restaurant syndrome is the ingredient is  now seeing a comeback even in some  unusual ways all right so this first  one's just going to be a regular Lopez  all showed us how MSG can be used in  cocktails he set up this taste test  oh this is different I'm gonna guess  that this is the one with MSG in it  correct I get that savory flavor you  were talking about back at lucky danger  Ma's slogan is American Chinese by a  Chinese American this is really taking  the dishes that have been Americanized  like Kung Pao chicken General Tso's and  just updating it some people would say  it might just be easier for you to avoid  the whole thing and not use MSG but then  the food won't be as good it's a very  necessary ingredient for us  so look MSG may not be for everyone and  that's okay but now some of the stigmas  about MSG are Vanishing and people are  starting to realize hey this is pretty  good and it works in scrambled eggs even  apple pie what apple pie you think  Savory not sweet it tends to be better  in Savory food but folks some folks are  sensitive to it absolutely and I think  the big takeaway here is if you are  sensitive to it sure steer clear it's  good to think of it like Dairy some  people are lactose intolerant and they  should avoid Dairy but that doesn't mean  that Dairy is dangerous or unsafe it's  the same with MSG yeah isn't it just  particular to the person eating right  just like you said gluten or other  ingredients that you might be sensitive  to absolutely there are sensitivities  out there and and just kind of trust  your own system and just say hey this  doesn't really sit well with me olive  oil well if it's in Chick-fil-A it's  good enough for me and Doritos too  absolutely  appreciate
 

